            <section class="faq-section">
                <h2>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>
                <div class="faq-item">
                    <h4>What are the most common propellants used in automotive aerosols?</h4>
                    <p>The most common propellants are liquefied petroleum gas (LPG) due to its high pressure and cost-effectiveness, and carbon dioxide (CO2) for non-flammable applications like specialized brake cleaners. Dimethyl Ether (DME) is also used for its unique solvent properties.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="faq-item">
                    <h4>How do I ensure my automotive aerosol is safe for sensitive electronics?</h4>
                    <p>Safety for electronics is achieved by using high dielectric strength solvents that do not conduct electricity and leave zero residue. Formulations must be tested for compatibility with various plastics and rubbers common in modern vehicle connectors.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="faq-item">
                    <h4>Is contract manufacturing a viable option for small startups?</h4>
                    <p>Absolutely. Contract manufacturing allows startups to access professional-grade R&D and production without the capital investment of building a factory. It is the fastest and most reliable way to scale an aerosol brand.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="faq-item">
                    <h4>What is the typical shelf life of an automotive aerosol product?</h4>
                    <p>When formulated correctly and housed in high-quality tinplate or aluminium cans, most automotive aerosols have a shelf life of 2 to 5 years, provided they are stored in a cool, dry environment away from direct sunlight.</p>
